; 28-prove-with-modal-logic.kurt
;
; Prove with modal logic
;
; modal.kurt is the classical normal modal logic T: formulas built from
; propositional letters with `¬`, `∧`, `∨`, `→`, `⇔`, `□` (necessarily) and
; `◇` (possibly). A line `⊢ A` says that the formula A is a theorem of T --
; each step below is one, by an axiom of T or by one of its rules, modus ponens
; and necessitation.

load modal

const P, Q
formula P, Q           ; P and Q are propositional letters

; An axiom: `A → (B → A)`, here for P and Q.

⊢ (P → (Q → P))

; Necessitation: from a theorem A follows the theorem □A.

⊢ □(P → (Q → P))

; A theorem is what follows from no assumptions: `empty ⊢ A` says the same
; as `⊢ A`.

empty ⊢ □(P → (Q → P))

; T is classical, and it has the axiom T, `□A → A` (what is necessary is
; true), and its dual `A → ◇A`.

⊢ (P ∨ ¬P)
⊢ (□P → P)
⊢ (P → ◇P)

; □ distributes over ∧, and ◇ is ¬□¬.

⊢ ((□P ∧ □Q) ⇔ □(P ∧ Q))
⊢ (◇P ⇔ ¬□¬P)

; Modus ponens: from the theorems A and A → B follows the theorem B. Here A is
; the first theorem of this lesson, and A → B the axiom from above for it.

⊢ ((P → (Q → P)) → (Q → (P → (Q → P))))
⊢ (Q → (P → (Q → P)))
